program            = 006_enum_definitions
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = Enumerator value assignment, gaps, negative enumerators and the int range edges, with switch lowering over a sparse label set
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
No signed overflow occurs anywhere, including at the int range edges. B_LOW is written as
-2147483647 - 1 rather than as -2147483648 precisely so that every intermediate value is an
int-typed constant within int range: the literal 2147483648 would not fit in int and would
acquire a wider type, whereas 2147483647 fits and subtracting 1 from its negation reaches the
int minimum with no step outside the range. B_HIGH is the int maximum written directly. Both
edge values are only printed, never used as arithmetic operands, so no computation approaches
either edge. The arithmetic that is performed uses only small enumerators: COLOR_BLUE plus S_B
is 13 and S_D minus S_A is 11, both far from the range edges. The cast (enum sparse)selector
converts the value 20, which is exactly the enumerator S_C and therefore within the
enumeration's value range, so the conversion is value preserving. classify handles all four
enumerators with case labels and additionally returns -1 after the switch, so its control flow
is well defined for any argument value and control can never fall off the end of the function.
Every object read is initialized: selector has an initializer and folded, runtime and arith are
all assigned at their declarations. No shift and no pointer arithmetic is performed. No object
is modified twice between sequence points, and no printf call receives a side-effecting
argument -- both classify results are assigned to locals on separate statements first, so
unspecified evaluation order cannot affect the output. There is no aliasing violation and no
padding byte is read.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
ENUMERATOR VALUES ARE ALL WITHIN INT RANGE, BY REQUIREMENT. C11 permits an implementation to
choose any integer type capable of representing all of an enumeration's values as that
enumeration's compatible type. Once an enumerator exceeds int range that choice becomes
implementation-defined, and the four backends could then legitimately differ -- which would
produce a cross-backend divergence that is not a defect and would poison oracle (b). Every
enumerator in this program is therefore kept strictly inside int range. The extremes are
exactly the int minimum (B_LOW) and the int maximum (B_HIGH), and no other enumerator comes
near either.

NO RAW ENUM SIZE IS PRINTED. The compatible type of an enumeration is implementation-defined
even for values within int range, so the program prints only the relation sizeof(enum color)
== sizeof(int) as a 0 or 1 value, never a raw size. Every enumerator is also explicitly cast
to int before printing, which normalizes the printed value and keeps the %d conversion correct
on every target regardless of the compatible type chosen.

REMAINING WIDTH NORMALIZATION. No sizeof of long, pointer, double, long long or any aggregate
containing them is printed, so the measured i686 difference (4-byte long and pointer versus 8
bytes on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64) cannot surface. No address or pointer value is printed.
Plain char is not used, so the measured char signedness difference (signed on x86_64 and i686,
unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) is irrelevant. The only printf conversion specifier used in
the program is %d.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
color=0 1 2
sparse=10 11 20 21
signed=-5 0 7 8
bounded_low_high=-2147483648 2147483647
arith=13 11
classify_folded=300 classify_runtime=300
enum_size_eq_int=1
END
